t(40, 955, """An angry person commented, objecting to my saying that it is not permissible to say of a dead man that he is being called to account now

Learn the answer and do not sanctify humans; the owner of "Khayriyya" is not made holy by his ignorance!

The answer: It is not permissible for a person to assert with certainty that a particular individual is being called to account now in a particular way. Among the scholars this is called "swearing upon Allah" (ta'alli). It is reported in the authentic hadith that a man said: "By Allah, Allah will not forgive so-and-so," so Allah the Mighty and Majestic said: "Who is this who swears upon Me that I will not forgive so-and-so? I have forgiven so-and-so and made your deeds void."

In summary: It is not sound in the Shari'ah to "assert" with certainty the details of what happens to the dead, because they are of the unseen. The right word is to pray for steadfastness and mercy for him, and to avoid expressions that suggest knowledge of Allah's judgment on His servants.""")

h(40, 1900, """Objection from an angry person: Swearing upon Allah, in someone's saying: So-and-so is being called to account now!""")

h(41, 600, """{They plotted their plot, and their plot is with Allah, even though their plot were such as to move mountains. So never think that Allah will break His promise to His messengers. Indeed Allah is Mighty, Lord of retribution} (46-47)""")

